 it the House of Lords  according to Baroness Meacher is  standing up for working people well that  would be a first though John is that we  cannot have a situation where the  unelected house appears over rules the  democratically elected House of Commons  the House of Lords has resisted that  temptation for a hundred years they have  had that temptation placed in their path  on dozens of occasions over the last  century  on tonight's votes particularly I might  add on the Labour motion not the  crossbench motion but particularly on  the Labour motion it is a constitutional  outrage this clearly involves four and a  half four point four billion pounds  worth of public spending forget for a  moment the issue that is at stake in  terms of the the pinpoint precision of  the amendments the principle that the  House of Commons holds sway over  financial matters is a crucial one to  the functioning of our Constitution and  otherwise we have self-appointed people  in the House of Lords people who have  decided that they know better than the  democratic elected elected chamber  attempting to overrule the elected  chamber but they've resisted that for a  hundred years tonight they haven't so  what we expect now is that the  government will bring a motion back to  the House of Commons do you think the  government can get it through in the  comments because we know there are all  these conservative backbenchers who are  unhappy with what the government's  proposing which on the point is it's  already passed to the House of Commons I  DS got the cottage cheese or Commons now  isn't it it was passed by the House of  Commons when conservative back benches  so they just didn't understand what it  was all about  and the fact is as Jacob Riis monk says  Conservative MP the huh cigars deals  with these regulations very badly  actually they they're busy people they  don't have time to go through these  things indeed it's not for the House of  Lords to make that judgment it is an  impertinence if you like for the House  of Lords to say that the House of  Commons doesn't know what it's doing you  may not like it but it's happened well  of course of course it's happened but  the point that I'm making John is that  it is unprecedented that it is an  outrage to the Constitution this  statutory instrument only five have been  defeated in the past hundred years and  all five of those none of them involved  finance so we've got a situation where  the House of Lords says it knows better  than the House of Commons we hear lines  such as the MPs didn't really know what  they were doing and things of that sort  Jacob is a friend of mine but you can  find members of parliament on any party  who will say different things what has  happened is what counts is the the House  of Commons has passed this and now it  has been sent to the House of Lords and  they have rejected it they've decided to  defy convention and it should and  hopefully will have consequences for the  House of Lords but what appears to have  happened here is that despite the  arguments that you're making about the  Constitution the fact is the lords seem  to be reflecting public opinion we've  done polls at Sky News others have done  them and you heard Tory MPs speak out in  the debate in the House of Commons last  week well we have a general election  system in this country where a party of  government is elected in this case  that's happened less than six months ago  it isn't for the House of Lords to to  subvert that and to say that our opinion  today matters more than the  democratically elected members apartment  John MacDonald has just made the point  on Sky News that in fact it was not in  the manifesto this specific proposal and  both Mr Cameron and Michael Gove when he  was the Chief Whip denied that this was  going to happen  well the Salisbury Convention which goes  back to 1945 is the manifesto I'm not  sure people care about themselves well  but I don't care about how much money's